---
mm: 0.1
kind: doc
name: modules.cpp C++20 language specification
---

# Purpose
This document specifies the subset of C++20 language and standard library
features permitted in modules.cpp source code. It restates docs/modules.mdy's
Self-contained and Inspectable design goals as concrete, checkable rules, so
that the codebase stays small, auditable, and free of exception-based control
flow and hand-written template or metaprogramming machinery.

# Conformance language
This specification uses MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY with the
following fixed meanings.

- MUST / MUST NOT — an absolute requirement or prohibition. Code that
  violates a MUST rule does not conform to this specification and is
  rejected in review, regardless of author intent.
- SHOULD / SHOULD NOT — a strong recommendation. Deviating requires a
  written justification in the code review or a comment at the point of
  deviation.
- MAY — fully optional; the choice is left to the author.

# Scope
This specification applies to all C++ source under modules/, tools/, and
apps/. Code under tests/ and modules/*/tests/ is held to the same rules
except where the Known non-conformance section below records an explicit,
bounded exception.

# Baseline compiler and standard
- Code MUST compile as ISO C++20, using only language and library features a
  conforming C++20 compiler and standard library provide.
- Code MUST NOT depend on compiler-specific extensions beyond the module
  support flag already documented as a current boundary in docs/modules.mdy
  (GCC's -fmodules-ts).
- Code MUST NOT depend on any third-party library, header-only or otherwise.
  This restates docs/modules.mdy's Self-contained design goal as a hard rule.

# Permitted language features
- export module, module, and import MUST be used for all project code;
  #include MUST be confined to the global module fragment (module;) and used
  only for standard library headers.
- Ordinary classes and structs, with constructors, destructors, and ordinary
  non-template member functions, MAY be used freely.
- A single level of virtual dispatch (a base class with a virtual destructor
  and virtual member functions, and concrete classes that override them) MAY
  be used where the architecture already relies on it, as with App. New
  polymorphic hierarchies beyond this pattern SHOULD be justified in review
  rather than introduced by default.
- enum class MUST be used in place of unscoped enum for all new enumerated
  types.
- [[nodiscard]] SHOULD be applied to any function whose return value must
  not be silently discarded.
- Non-capturing or by-reference lambdas MAY be used for short, local
  callbacks, such as a closing action passed to a single call site. Lambdas
  MUST NOT be stored as std::function or otherwise used as a substitute for
  a named function or for virtual dispatch.
- std::source_location::current() MAY be used as a default argument to
  attach call-site information to diagnostics, following mm.test's existing
  use.
- RAII MUST be used for every owned resource, including files, temporary
  directories, and file handles. Manual new and delete MUST NOT appear in
  project code.
- Copy and move special member functions MUST be declared explicitly,
  whether defaulted, deleted, or defined, for any type that owns a resource
  or must not be copied. Implicit generation MUST NOT be relied upon for
  such types.

# Disallowed: exceptions
- throw, try, and catch MUST NOT appear in project code outside the Known
  non-conformance section below.
- Functions MUST report failure through their return type: a status enum
  following the existing Enter::ok / Enter::skip / Enter::error pattern, a
  bool, or a std::error_code out-parameter for functions wrapping operating
  system or filesystem calls that themselves report errors that way.
- Standard library operations that can throw on their own terms, such as a
  container allocation failure, are unaffected by this rule; it governs
  throw and catch sites this project authors, not the exception
  specifications of the standard library itself.

# Disallowed: templates and metaprogramming
- Project code MUST NOT declare new template functions, template classes, or
  template aliases.
- Project code MUST NOT use compile-time dispatch or metaprogramming
  facilities such as concepts, requires-expressions, std::enable_if, if
  constexpr on types, variadic templates, or template specialization.
- Project code MAY instantiate and use templates already provided by the
  C++20 standard library, such as std::vector, std::map, std::string, and
  std::optional, exactly as the standard library specifies them. Using a
  standard container or algorithm is not authoring a template; writing a new
  one is what this rule prohibits.
- constexpr functions and variables MAY be used for compile-time constants
  and simple compile-time-evaluable functions. consteval MAY be used where a
  value must be guaranteed to evaluate at compile time. Neither MAY be
  combined with template parameters to perform compile-time dispatch.

# Disallowed: macros and the preprocessor
- Project code MUST NOT define object-like or function-like macros with
  #define, and MUST NOT #undef one.
- Project code MUST NOT use conditional compilation (#if, #ifdef, #ifndef,
  #elif, #else, #endif). The one documented compiler-specific dependency
  (GCC's -fmodules-ts, see "Baseline compiler and standard") is a build
  flag, not something project code branches on at preprocessor time; no
  code in this project may branch at preprocessor time on platform,
  compiler, or configuration.
- Project code MUST NOT use the stringizing (#) or token-pasting (##)
  preprocessor operators, and MUST NOT generate code through repeated
  macro expansion (an "X-macro" list or similar).
- Project code MUST NOT invoke a macro the standard library defines, such
  as assert() from <cassert>, and MUST NOT rely on predefined macros such
  as __FILE__, __LINE__, or __func__. std::source_location::current() is
  the normative replacement for call-site information (see "Permitted
  language features"), and failure MUST be reported through a function's
  return type (see "Disallowed: exceptions"), not asserted away.
- #include MUST remain the only preprocessor directive appearing in
  project code, confined to the global module fragment as already
  required by "Permitted language features", and used only to pull in
  standard library headers. A standard library header MAY itself define
  macros internally; this rule governs what project code writes and
  invokes, not the standard library's own implementation.

# Standard library usage
- std::filesystem MUST be used for path and filesystem operations, using the
  std::error_code overloads wherever an operation can fail. mm.build and the
  test scaffolding already use this pattern correctly, and it is normative
  going forward.
- std::vector MUST be the default sequential container. std::map MAY be used
  where key lookup is genuinely required. Other standard containers MAY be
  introduced when a vector or map is a clearly poor fit, with the choice
  justified in review.
- std::string_view MUST be preferred over std::string for non-owning,
  read-only string parameters.
- A plain function-pointer type alias, following mm.test's
  using fn = void (*)(); pattern, MUST be preferred over std::function for
  simple, non-capturing callback registration, to avoid the associated type
  erasure and heap allocation.

# Known non-conformance
This section records existing code that predates this specification and
does not yet conform. It is the only place a MUST violation is permitted,
and only for the entries listed here.

- mm.test's expect and failure use throw, and run_all catches them, which
  conflicts with the exceptions rule above. This is deliberate and
  load-bearing: it is the mechanism by which a failed assertion unwinds out
  of a test body without the test author writing manual control flow. It is
  the one permitted exception to the exceptions rule, confined to mm.test,
  until a non-throwing mechanism replaces it. No other module MAY introduce
  throw or catch on the strength of this precedent.
- mm.test's registrar constructor template, declared as
  template <std::size_t N> registrar(std::string_view name, const case_
  (&cases)[N]), is a hand-written template and conflicts with the templates
  rule above. It MAY remain until replaced by a non-template mechanism, such
  as an explicit count parameter at each call site, tracked as technical
  debt rather than used as precedent elsewhere.
- apps/mdy/mdy.cpp's explicit template specialization MUST be reviewed and
  either removed or added to this section with the same justification given
  for the two mm.test entries above.

# Enforcement
- Code review MUST check new and changed code against this specification
  before merge. A MUST violation outside the Known non-conformance list MUST
  block merge.
- ./check.sh runs tools/check, which wraps cppcheck with the addon at
  tools/check/cppcheck/cpp20_rules.py, over every source file the manifest
  tree declares plus tools/build/main.cpp (deliberately unmanifested; see
  docs/modules.mdy). This is selected syntactic enforcement, not full
  coverage of this document: the addon currently checks throw/try/catch,
  new template declarations, manual new/delete, std::function, enum (must
  be enum class), any preprocessor directive other than #include, and
  assert()/__FILE__/__LINE__/__func__, against the files above. It does
  not check, for example, [[nodiscard]], explicit special members,
  string_view preferences, filesystem error_code-overload usage, include
  or global-module-fragment placement, the stringizing/token-pasting
  operators or macro-generated code (harder to isolate on rawTokens than a
  directive line; see cpp20_rules.py), manual array delete (delete[] arr;
  is not matched by check_delete; see its comment in cpp20_rules.py), or
  whether a source file exists outside the manifest tree entirely.
  Extending coverage means adding a check function to cpp20_rules.py;
  there is no tracking issue for the gap beyond this paragraph.
- Adding an entry to Known non-conformance requires the same justification
  this document gives for its existing entries: what the exception buys, and
  what would need to happen to remove it.
